the relationship between the u.s and  saudi arabia has been a bit of a roller  coaster this is obviously a challenging  time we've become very good friends they  have to be held accountable as a  presidential candidate joe biden pledged  to punish saudi arabia for its human  rights abuses they're going in and  murdering children and they're murdering  innocent people and he vowed to make  them in fact the pariah that they are  but after major geopolitical shifts in  recent months things might be about to  change the white house formally  announced that biden will visit oil rich  saudi arabia with a meeting between  president biden and saudi crown prince  mohammed bin salman on the horizon what  could the two leaders work toward and at  what cost  here are five key things that are likely  to come up in talks first up oil prices  which have been soaring due to sanctions  on russian oil exports and a supply  demand imbalance created by the pandemic  difficult times for americans across the  u.s as gas prices continue to rise the  average price of gas hit a painful five  dollars a gallon saudi arabia might be  able to help at least to a certain  extent it's one of the world's biggest  oil producers and a leading member of  opec plus a coalition of countries  including russia that controls more than  half of the world's oil exports so far  saudi arabia has largely resisted calls  by the u.s and its european allies to  pump more oil instead sticking to a deal  blessed by russia  but then in june opec plus agreed to up  production by a few thousand barrels a  day it's not a huge amount but it did  draw some praise from the white house  now with a visit biden who is under  pressure to lower prices at the pump  could address the topic in a meeting  since saudi arabia is one of the few  countries in the world capable of  boosting production by a significant  amount  another issue at the top of the list is  security saudi arabia wants greater  military aid and security assurances  from the united states since 2015 the  country has been leading a bombing  campaign in neighboring yemen against  the iran-backed houthi movement the  houthis have been fighting for greater  political sway in yemen and have  retaliated by launching attacks on  targets in saudi arabia  in years past the us provided saudi  arabia with billions of dollars of  military aid  but over the years the saudi-led  campaign has resulted in thousands of  civilian deaths damaged infrastructure  and widespread hunger prompting  president biden to halt the flow of  certain offensive weapons we're not  going to in fact sell more weapons to  them but i would end and the subsidies  that we have and the sale of material to  the saudis recently saudi arabia  extended a truce in yemen a move sought  by the u.s this fragile truce offers the  best hope in years for ending what the  un has called the world's worst  humanitarian crisis  that could pave the way for the u.s to  send more military aid to saudi arabia  u.s and saudi leaders could also work  toward normalizing relations between  saudi arabia and israel officially saudi  arabia doesn't have formal diplomatic  relations with its regional neighbor and  it supports the creation of an  independent palestinian state but in  secret meetings saudi arabia has been  moving toward eventual ties with israel  to further that process the biden  administration has been working to  broker agreements between the two  countries like allowing more commercial  flights from israel to fly over saudi  airspace and transferring ownership of  two red sea islands to saudi arabia a  move that requires israel's blessing  those would be two pretty significant  steps in the direction of normalization  president biden and his team have been  working on this they do see this as a  u.s interest though it might still be  years away normalized relations between  the two countries would reshape politics  in the middle east and it could give  other arab and muslim nations a green  light to recognize israel  next up the leaders may also discuss the  iran nuclear crisis iran has been moving  forward on its nuclear program and  recently it removed cameras monitoring  its work the head of the international  atomic energy agency says this could  deal a quote fatal blow to the iran  nuclear agreement the white house hasn't  yet given up on a revived iran nuclear  deal which was designed to keep tabs on  what iran was producing in order to  prevent it from working on a covert  weapons program but saudi arabia and  some of its neighbors opposed the deal  for fear that it would embolden iran's  leaders finally biden is under pressure  to address the kingdom's human rights  record in a face-to-face meeting with  prince muhammad human rights groups and  saudi dissidents living abroad have  already criticized him for planning to  meet with the very man u.s intelligence  agencies concluded ordered the operation  that killed washington post journalist  jamal khashoggi we will not move on  gemma's legacy will live on  no matter the normal lasik not  normalization efforts the crown prince  has denied having any prior knowledge of  the killing u.s officials across the  political spectrum have also criticized  prince muhammad warning about other  human rights abuses like the jailing of  political opponents the alleged torture  of women's rights activists and mass  executions  many are calling on biden to use his  leverage to demand concrete change  before any meeting takes place i'm not  going to change my view on human rights  but as president united states my job is  to bring peace if i can  clearly the u.s and saudi arabia have a  lot of issues they can work on but many  fear that without demanding action on  human rights issues president biden will  simply enable prince muhammad to  continue ruling with impunity so what do  you think the biden administration  should press for if anything leading up  to the saudi arabia trip leave a comment  below and if you like this video hit the  subscribe button for more stories like  this i'll see you next time  [Music]
 

